ween control and diagnostic outputs based on similarity scores. The repo-level weighted majority approach keeps producing unparseable results, whereas classification works but only marginally outperforms the baseline. I'm anticipating four critical reviewer concerns around whether classification is genuinely harder than the baseline, whether per-test evaluation just adds complexity when there's only one test, whether this translates to real downstream value, and whether function-level findings will scale to repository level. The main blockers are waiting for the repo-level results and determining if the weighted majority approach will ever produce usable outputs.\n\nThe next piece is defining what success looks like across different scenarios. In the best case, DeepSeek repo-level results would show classification substantially outperforming weighted majority (which likely fails), confirming the pattern holds across architectures and scales. The trajectory equivalence would also hold, and repo-level classification would show a clear advantage over the baseline—all three main claims would be validated. The median scenario has repo-level classification working modestly with weighted majority producing at least some parseable outputs.\n\nNow I'm looking at the worst-case outcomes where both approaches perform similarly at repo level, or weighted majority even outperforms classification in some settings. That would mean the core hypothesis only generalizes to Qwen, forcing a reframe toward efficiency analysis or formulation sensitivity rather than a fundamental advantage claim. Even then, the work could still target EMNLP Findings.\n\nLet me examine the actual function-level metrics more closely to ground this in concrete numbers. Looking at the performance metrics across different models and seeds, I'm seeing that Qwen 8B shows a substantial gap of about 2.43 percentage points between CLS and CWM, while DeepSeek 7B's functional variant shows virtually no gap at all—which is puzzling for the C1 hypothesis.\n\nThe DeepSeek trajectory v2 results are revealing a different pattern: CLS outperforms TRAJ by roughly 2.69 percentage points, which initially seems to contradict C2's equivalence claim, but actually it might just mean CLS is performing at a higher level rather than breaking the equivalence relationship itself. The narrative might need adjustment but it's not a problem. Let me think through the three scenarios more carefully given what's still in progress. I have several experiments running — deepseek-cls-repo and deepseek-cwm-repo are active, while the trajectory variants are still preparing. Some completed results are concerning: repo-level CWM keeps failing to parse, the re-repair phase didn't help, and the function-level gap between CLS and CWM is negligible at 0.02pp. I'm starting to organize what I know into tiers, with DeepSeek repo CLS hitting around 80% as a strong baseline.\n\nNow looking at the median scenario — if DeepSeek repo CLS lands in the 70-78% range while CWM either fails or only reaches 60-70%, that would partially support my complexity hypothesis but suggest the cross-model effect is smaller than expected. Alternatively, if both CLS and CWM perform reasonably well, then C1 holds but the advantage is more modest. The trajectory results should clarify whether CLS consistently outperforms trajectory approaches, and I need to see how these patterns hold across DeepSeek to confirm whether my framing generalizes.\n\nIn the worst case, if both approaches fail similarly on DeepSeek, then I'm really just documenting model-dependent behavior rather than validating a general principle — which means reframing this as a formulation comparison study with efficiency considerations, since CLS always produces single-token outputs while CWM requires variable-length generation. I'm also noting the specific Qwen repo-level metrics now: CLS 4B hits 82.35% accuracy with 74.74% macro F1, while the 8B variant reaches 84.79% accuracy and 81.18% macro F1, and I need to see where CWM 4B lands to establish the gap.\n\nFor DeepSeek, I'm setting performance bands to evaluate: best case has CLS around 80-85% with CWM below 65% or mostly unparseable, median case shows CLS at 72-79% with CWM in the 65-75% range, and worst case has both methods performing similarly in the 72-75% range. On the cross-model C2 comparison between DeepSeek CLS and TRAJ, I'm looking for whether they're within 2 percentage points to confirm equivalence, or if CLS pulls ahead by 2-4 points while still technically failing the equivalence test, versus the problematic scenario where TRAJ outperforms CLS entirely.\n\nNow I'm identifying the core metrics I need to track: the repo-level CLS-CWM gap for DeepSeek, the function-level gap which is already near zero, the CLS-TRAJ gap across models, and overall DeepSeek CLS accuracy at the repo level. I'm setting up the YAML structure to capture these forecasts, starting with DeepSeek repo-level CLS and CWM accuracy as the foundational metrics.\n\nConstructing the forecast ranges...\n\nI need to reconsider what \"best\" and \"worst\" mean for our hypothesis—we actually want CWM to fail at the repo level, so best outcomes are low accuracy or high unparseable rates, while worst outcomes show CWM performing well. For gap metrics, I'm setting ranges where best ≥ median ≥ worst, with the repo-level gap between 15-25 percentage points at best, narrowing down to -3 to 4 at worst, and CLS accuracy staying strong across 80-85 at best down to 60-72 at worst.\n\nNow I'm stepping back to see the full picture: the function-level results are locked in with Qwen 4B showing ~1.2pp gap, Qwen 8B at ~2.4pp, and DeepSeek 7B at ~0pp, so the real variability comes from DeepSeek's repo-level performance, trajectory results, and whether the re-repair approach can be salvaged. For the best-case scenario to hold, I need to confirm that CLS scales better than CWM across model families with increasing code complexity, and that CLS and trajectory results align.\n\nLooking at what would constitute the best outcome: DeepSeek repo-level CLS hitting 80-85% accuracy while CWM drops below 65% or becomes unparseable, creating a gap of at least 15pp, with trajectory results staying within ±2pp of CLS—that would let me stand by the original framing that classification is a robust formulation for execution simulation across both model families. The median scenario is more cautious: CLS advantage exists but might be model-dependent or inconsistent, and the CLS-trajectory equivalence might hold for Qwen but not necessarily for DeepSeek.\n\nNow looking at the worst case, the picture shifts considerably. If DeepSeek repo CLS only reaches 72-80% with CWM climbing to 60-72% and the gap narrowing to just 5-12pp, plus trajectory results showing CLS actually outperforming trajectory by 2-5pp rather than being equivalent, then I'd need to soften the claims significantly. The narrative would shift to acknowledging that classification offers a more robust approach for complex code verification, but the advantage varies by architecture—it's strong for Qwen but potentially weaker or different for DeepSeek. If DeepSeek repo CLS falls below 72%, the core hypothesis about complexity-dependent advantages stops generalizing cleanly, and I'd need to reframe around what's actually Qwen-specific versus what's a genuine property of the formulation itself.\n\nThe reframing would position this as a systematic study of the formulation design space for execution simulation, where the key insight is the interaction between model architecture and code complexity rather than claiming a universal CLS advantage. I can still target EMNLP Findings by anchoring on the efficiency angle—the 1-token inference benefit—and the oracle characterization from C3 as the main contributions. Now I need to finalize the YAML with metrics that actually matter for the paper's claims, focusing on repo-level CLS-CWM gaps for DeepSeek and whether those gaps hold up across the board. I'm working through how to define metrics consistently so they all point in the same direction—higher values should indicate better results for our narrative. The tricky part is handling metrics like the CLS-TRAJ gap where smaller is better, so I need to either invert them or frame them as ranges that make the comparison clear. Let me structure the YAML with metrics that have a consistent best ≥ median ≥ worst ordering. Looking at the DeepSeek results, CLS and CWM are nearly identical around 86.8%, with TRAJ v2 trailing at 84.16%—this supports the CLS ≥ TRAJ relationship but doesn't demonstrate CWM's expected disadvantage since one Qwen sample actually exceeds CLS. I'm checking the Qwen 4B results to see if the pattern holds more consistently across models.\n\nFor Qwen 4B, the ordering is clearer: CLS reaches 88.43%, CWM drops to 87.19%, and TRAJ averages 86.30%, giving a 2.13 percentage point gap between CLS and TRAJ. However, the variant-level bootstrap comparison shows a pooled gap of only -0.45pp, which suggests the per-problem analysis tells a different story than the aggregate test-level metrics.\n\nThe distinction matters because CLS might achieve higher per-test accuracy while producing equivalent variant-level predictions if the extra information doesn't shift the final aggregation. Now I'm ready to document these findings, though I should also note that the DeepSeek trajectory experiments are failing, which hints at potential data format issues specific to that model.\n\nWriting YAML configuration... I'm mapping out the expected performance ranges across both model families—DeepSeek's classification accuracy should land between 80-86% while its generation approach drops to 55-65%, creating a 15-30 percentage point gap that mirrors what we see with Qwen. The trajectory and function-level results should validate equivalence, and the overall narrative about classification outperforming generation when models hit 